martinmunk-avr-controld (0.5.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * shairport watcher: replay the last known AirPlay volume on the play edge.
    Senders set their volume before PlayerState flips to Playing and volume is
    ignored while idle, so sessions started at whatever level the receiver
    remembered (full-scale input). Verified on VærkstedsTærten: sender 15% ->
    Opalum 15 at power-on, source Mixer, off 30 s after stop.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:21:38 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.5.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New Opalum backend (backend=opalum) on the opalum-receiver library:
    power/volume/mute/source + auto_standby (sta0/sta1). Write-only RS232
    protocol, so state is what the daemon last set -- the daemon must be the
    port's only owner. Deployed on VærkstedsTærten in place of the ALSA
    mixer plugin + avr-power hooks (shairport ignore_volume_control=yes,
    the daemon drives the Opalum from the D-Bus AirPlay volume, ceiling 50).
  * venv bundles opalum-receiver 0.1.0 from the Forgejo PyPI registry.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:15:21 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.3) stable; urgency=medium

  * NAD: the receiver's own auto-standby timer was powering it off under a
    live TV (~30 min of silence) while the power policy thought it was on.
    Exposed as a switch (Main.AutoStandby) and turned OFF at startup whenever
    power_policy is on (auto_standby = keep opts out).
  * NAD: every read is name-checked; unsolicited Variable=Value echoes from
    remote/front panel (previously flushed, or misread as a query's answer)
    are drained into push events and logged.
  * Power policy: tracks the receiver's actual power; an external OFF
    releases ownership so the next demand edge powers it on again, an
    external ON is adopted only while a demand is active.
  * NAD: expose the remaining T757 RS232 variables (tone defeat, speaker
    trims, subwoofer, tuner, video mode, serial, per-source config, preset
    names) with HA entity categories; static ones read at announce only.
  * MQTT bridge: log every incoming command.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:03:22 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * Remove reverse volume sync (receiver -> phone slider). shairport won't relay
    volume to the AirPlay sender in the NAD-owns-volume setup, so the push was a
    no-op that only produced misleading "pushed to phone" logs and extra serial
    polling. Backlogged. Forward (phone -> NAD) and input-stolen handling are
    unchanged.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:45:00 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * feed_demand: when a source demand clears while the receiver stays on for
    another demand, restore that demand's input (e.g. stop AirPlay to Main while
    the TV is on -> switch Main back to the TV input).
  * Packaging: start/restart the service on install and upgrade (was left stopped
    after apt upgrade). The unit is gated on ConditionPathExists=the config, so a
    fresh install without config does not crash-loop.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:10:00 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * First release of the Python avr-controld: a broker daemon that owns each AV
    receiver connection and serves the ALSA plugins and avr-cec-relay over the
    same Unix-socket protocol as the C++ daemon, plus a Home Assistant MQTT
    bridge. Shipped as a bundled virtualenv (arch: all; pure-Python deps).
  * NAD backend (RS232, T7xx) exposing power/volume/mute/source plus extras
    (bass/treble, Zone 2 power/volume/mute/source, sleep, tone, model/firmware).
  * Multi-zone AirPlay: one shairport-sync watcher per zone over per-zone D-Bus
    (zones= config), each mapped to a receiver zone (Main core, or Zone 2 via
    extras). Phone slider -> zone volume (clamped to a per-zone ceiling) and
    reverse (receiver remote/front panel -> phone slider via SetAirplayVolume).
  * Power policy per demand (AirPlay playing, CEC/TV active) with off-grace;
    per-zone power grace; drop the AirPlay session when the receiver input is
    switched away from a zone. Demands act on activation edges so a client may
    re-assert them (survives a daemon restart).
  * Serial access is finely locked so a long state sweep never blocks interactive
    commands (CEC/phone volume); volume-signal bursts are coalesced.
  * Offline unit tests (no hardware/MQTT/serial) run hermetically in CI.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <hansen@martinmunk.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 11:35:00 +0000
